<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Cheng of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1042–1021 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Cheng of Zhou was the second king of the Western Zhou, son of King Wu. Because he came to the throne young, his uncle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> ruled as regent, crushing a major rebellion and consolidating Zhou rule in the east. Tradition credits his reign with the enfeoffment of many regional states, among them Jin and, by Chu tradition, Chu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-cheng-of-zhou.jpg" alt="King Cheng of Zhou" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Cheng_of_Zhou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
